# MCP RPC Verification-First Hardening

## Purpose

Use this workflow for UnrealMCP RPC performance, transport, queueing, timeout,
batch, job, cancellation, pagination, or persistent-connection work.

The governing rule is **verification before optimization**. No measured speedup
may hide an uncertain mutation, protocol regression, shutdown hazard, or legacy
client break. Each behavioral change is independently gated, feature-flagged,
and reversible until the combined soak passes.

## Safety Boundary

- Work from an isolated clean worktree based on the intended integration commit.
- Do not launch, close, build against, connect to, or otherwise disturb a shared
  Unreal Editor while another editor task is active.
- Offline tests, source inspection, and documentation are allowed during that
  window. UE build and live canary work wait for an explicitly safe editor window.
- Use only `/Game/_MCP_Temp/RPCVerification/...` for mutation canaries. Record
  pre/post state, dirty packages, cleanup, and retained artifacts.
- Do not retry a timed-out mutation automatically. A timeout is an observation
  failure, not proof of cancellation or rollback.
- Preserve the current one-command-per-connection protocol and full response
  schema as the compatibility fallback until a later removal is separately
  reviewed and approved.

## Model Routing

Model choice is an efficiency aid, never a substitute for a gate.

| Work | Default model | Required second look |
|---|---|---|
| Repository audit, benchmark extraction, external comparison, offline Python tests, docs, low-risk instrumentation | Terra | Sol when the result changes a P0/P1 design decision |
| Event/wakeup design, timeout ownership, bounded game-thread queue, job/cancel state machine, persistent framing, socket shutdown, UObject/GC lifetime | Sol | Another Sol review before promotion |
| Mechanical implementation after an approved design | Terra | Sol reviews concurrency-sensitive C++ diff |
| Release evidence and final Go/No-Go | Sol plus MCP Release Verifier role | All required automated and live gates |

The orchestrator should route work this way when the models are available. If a
model is unavailable, continue with the available model but do not relax any
test, review, or evidence requirement.

